          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Feathers for wings, collars, soft hackles. Beads for weight and a clean head. Shanks when the fly is articulated. Tools and adhesives sit next door as supplies.</p><h3>By water</h3><p>Trout: lighter hooks, softer fiber, smaller beads. Steelhead and salt: stronger wire, flash, materials that survive abrasive mouths.</p><h3>Skill ladder</h3><p><strong>Beginner</strong><br>Start with a vise and one materials chip: feathers or beads. Tie the same simple pattern until the wraps feel boring. Buy the next bag only when that bag is empty.</p><p><strong>Getting comfortable</strong><br>Add the category you keep running out of. If you fish trout nymphs, that is usually beads and dubbing. If you fish soft hackles, it is waterfowl. Stop filling every empty drawer.</p><p><strong>Advanced / steelhead\u2013salt</strong><br>Build around the patterns you actually fish. Stronger wire, flash, articulated shanks, materials that survive abrasive mouths. Specialty packs (Select wood duck, football beads) earn their keep when the pattern calls for them, not because the shelf looks full.</p><p>We stock what we tie with in Ilwaco. Brands on the shelf are there because they show up in our own patterns.</p>"
          }
        },
