    "bench_resources": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Bench resources",
            "heading_size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>These are the questions we get at the desk. Use them before you fill another drawer.</p><h3>Bead to hook (quick)</h3><p>Match bead OD to the hook eye and the sink rate you want. Too small and the fly never gets down. Too big and the head looks clumsy. Start in the middle of the size chart for that hook family, then go heavier only if the water asks for it.</p><h3>Thread</h3><p>Fine thread for small dry flies and clean heads. Stronger thread when you are packing deer hair, tying salt, or building articulated shanks. If the thread keeps cutting materials, step up a size before you blame the feather.</p><h3>Hooks</h3><p>Dry, nymph, jig, and saltwire are different jobs. Wire weight and gape matter more than the brand stamp on the box. Pick the hook for the water, then pull materials that fit that hook.</p><h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Feathers for wings and soft hackles. Beads when you need weight and a clean head. Shanks when the fly is articulated. Vises and scissors under Tools.</p>"
          }
        },
